---
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import GetChip from '../components/GetChip.astro';
---
<Shell
  current="sources"
  title="Sources · The Grid"
  description="The honest feed-health board for the Grid: per-source poll interval, last success, staleness, thresholds, and last error — live from GET /api/v1/sources. A failing feed says so; absence is never an all-clear."
>
  <!-- Page-scoped, but is:global because the rules target rows built at runtime
       by sources.js (scoped styles would not match dynamically-created DOM).
       Tokens are the canonical --st-* status colors from app.css. -->
  <style is:global slot="head">
    .data-table tr.row-STALE {
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-STALE) 8%, transparent);
    }
    .data-table tr.row-UNAVAILABLE {
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--st-UNAVAILABLE) 11%, transparent);
    }
    .data-table td.err-cell { color: var(--st-UNAVAILABLE); }
    /* Attribution sits under the source name; it is an obligation, not an id,
       so it is set apart from the id line above it. */
    .data-table .src-attr { color: var(--text-2nd); font-style: italic; max-width: 22rem; }
  </style>

  <PageHead kicker="System status" title="Sources">
    <p class="lead">Every upstream feed, reporting its own health.</p>
    <GetChip slot="badges" path="/api/v1/sources" />
  </PageHead>

  <!-- No "Feed health" section head. It was the only heading on the page and it
       restated the hero — "Sources / every upstream feed, reporting its own
       health" then "Feed health" — which made this the one screen that opens
       with two titles. Every other page goes hero → caption → data, and the
       head's `get` and `cap` had somewhere better to be: the request is a badge
       in the hero, the column order is the caption. -->
  <p class="rail-caption">
    <b>Board</b>one row per upstream feed, unhealthy first · poll → last success → freshness
  </p>

  <!-- SIDENOTES, not a rail. The board below is a wide table — six columns of
       per-source health — and a 300px rail would take a third of its width. A
       page whose data needs the full measure keeps it, and the asides move
       beside the prose instead. -->
  <div class="with-notes">
    <p class="section-intro">
      Every event and every layer traces to one of these feeds. For each you get the poll
      interval, the last successful fetch, how close it is to going
      <span class="mono">STALE</span>, the staleness thresholds and the last error verbatim —
      the honest-freshness contract, exposed. A feed that fails says so;
      <span class="accent-orange">absence of data is never shown as an all-clear.</span>
    </p>
    <aside class="sidenote">
      <div class="rail-label">Reading the board</div>
      <p>
        A source goes <span class="mono">STALE</span> once its last success is older than
        <code class="inline">staleAfterSeconds</code> — three poll intervals by default — and
        <span class="mono">UNAVAILABLE</span> on persistent failure.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Read the live values here rather than hard-coding intervals.</strong> They are
        per-source tunables and they change.</p>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <div id="summary-line" class="chip-row" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <div id="degradation-alert"></div>
  <div id="page-errors"></div>
  <div id="board"><div class="loading">Loading /api/v1/sources…</div></div>
  <p id="refresh-line" class="freshness"></p>

  <!-- The obligation that rides with the data. Stated here because this is the
       page that enumerates the feeds, and it is the one place a consumer sees
       every attribution string at once. The evacuation clause is life-safety,
       so it takes the accent label rather than sitting mid-paragraph. -->
  <div class="with-notes section-gap">
    <p class="section-intro">
      Display the <code class="inline">attribution</code> string wherever you render a
      source's data, and link the upstream. Every row above carries one.
    </p>
    <aside class="sidenote">
      <div class="rail-label alert">Evacuation is reference-only</div>
      <p>
        In every state, and it must always be shown with its link to the authoritative
        <a href="https://protect.genasys.com/search" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Genasys</a>
        viewer — a zone count from this API is never the instruction to act on.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <script is:inline type="module">
    import { initSourcesPage } from '/assets/pages/sources.js';
    initSourcesPage();
  </script>
</Shell>
